{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/229279-sin-eaters-as-externalized-conscience","id":229279,"title":"Sin-Eaters as externalized conscience","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Geist: The Sin-Eaters"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-08-15T11:29:04-07:00","id":229279,"title":"Sin-Eaters as externalized conscience","content":"So possibly nonsensical rambling ahead, you've been warned.<br /> <br /> I got to thinking about the bargain, and the way geist/bound interactions are written in the rules (namely, that the geist has almost no power to influence the decisions of the bound beyond whispering in their ear and possibly resurrecting them). Likewise, the fact that observing the geist's stated wishes doesn't seem to actually factor into Synergy at all. And I thought about why this is.<br /> <br /> And, I mean, one possibility is that it's just poorly written/designed. That's always a strong contender, but it's also the least interesting answer because it pretty much ends the discussion. So I think it's more interesting to assume for the sake of argument that this is working exactly the way it's supposed to.<br /> <br /> So assuming this is how the bargain is <i>supposed </i>to work, and that geists know that, why would a tiny rampant death god who mostly just wants to burn the world make a bargain like that?<br /> <br /> Well, geists used to be people, before they were death gods. And somewhere along the way they started losing their anchors, the things that tied them to the mortal world, and they started drifting down through the underworld into dissolution. But unlike most ghosts, they were driven or lucky enough to find a way to stick around, either by giving up parts of their personality to tie themselves to concepts and aspects of death instead of to their mortal lives, or else by merging with spirits (depending on which theory of geist formation you go for).<br /> <br /> Either way, this involved giving up some aspect of <i>who they were </i>in return for continued existence. They stopped being people, in a very real sense. The view that says that geists get shafted in the Sin-Eater bargain assumes that they were 100% okay with that transformation. That they were totally okay with going from, say, &quot;lady who died in a fire&quot; to &quot;Duchess of Scorched Flesh&quot;, and that the bargain is something they undertake specifically to further the goals of the <i>latter </i>entity.<br /> <br /> But what if that's not the case? What if our poor woman who died in a fire <i>dreads</i> becoming something not-herself? What if she wants a way to make sure she can't hurt anyone, to make sure that even if she becomes a terrifying monster that her ability to <i>do</i> anything about it will be severely curtailed?<br /> <br /> And if her unfinished business is important to her, maybe she dreads losing or burying the part of herself that cares about that, too.<br /> <br /> Enter the bargain. By ensuring beforehand that the only way she can really reliably interact with the mortal world is through an empowered <i>person </i>who presumably shares some of her values (or at least moreso than the thing she's about to become), she's sort of externalizing her conscience. Suddenly, it's okay that she's giving up the part of herself that recoils from the idea of burning infants, because she is going to be working with someone who will almost certainly say &quot;no, we're definitely not doing that&quot;. Her relative powerlessness in the bargain is therefore part of the design: if all she can do is whisper in her sin-eater's ear, then the sin-eater gets the final approval on all of her wants and desires, which hopefully means automatically discarding all the really awful ones, while giving in to the reasonable ones just to shut her up. She still gets to pursue some of what she wants in the world (by proxy), while all the terrible things she's planning to incorporate into herself are (hopefully) blocked by the sin-eater's humanity.<br /> <br /> Also, by binding herself to someone whose entire <i>schtick </i>probably involves seeing and helping ghosts, she's ensuring that even if <i>she</i> stops caring about her unfinished business, her bound partner is strongly inclined to treat her as a puzzle and try to figure it out and address it. By turning herself into an enigma and then leaving herself somewhere that a puzzle-hungry sin-eater is bound to start wondering about her, she's seeing to it that in the long run someone will care about that even if she doesn't.<br /> <br /> Basically, from one point of view the bargain is a long con run by the geist's past self against its current self, with the sin-eater as an instrument.<br /> <br /> Did this make any sense? And if so, thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","name":"Gabby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-08-15T12:10:22-07:00","id":229376,"title":"","content":"I kind of like this. Great idea!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-08-18T11:22:53-07:00","id":233063,"title":"","content":"There are problems with this theory that, I think, are really just problems with Geist itself.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gabby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/229279-sin-eaters-as-externalized-conscience#post229279\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, I mean, one possibility is that it's just poorly written/designed. That's always a strong contender, but it's also the least interesting answer because it pretty much ends the discussion. So I think it's more interesting to assume for the sake of argument that this is working exactly the way it's supposed to.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, I hate to say it but I really do think this is the reason. Geist is a bunch of great themes and great ideas mixed with piss poor mechanics and a severe lack of vision. But we'll continue on otherwise.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> why would a tiny rampant death god who mostly just wants to burn the world make a bargain like that? </div> </div> </div>Not all of them DO want to burn the world, though. In fact, most of the Sin-eaters we see have Geists who want the exact opposite. Lolly's Geist, The Candyman, would see overdoses as a severe abuse of the dealer-client relationship, and her Krewe spends it's time making sure everyone is doing drugs safely. <br /> <br /> Either way, this involved giving up some aspect of <i>who they were </i>in return for continued existence. They stopped being people, in a very real sense. The view that says that geists get shafted in the Sin-Eater bargain assumes that they were 100% okay with that transformation. That they were totally okay with going from, say, &quot;lady who died in a fire&quot; to &quot;Duchess of Scorched Flesh&quot;, and that the bargain is something they undertake specifically to further the goals of the <i>latter </i>entity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But what if that's not the case? What if our poor woman who died in a fire <i>dreads</i> becoming something not-herself? What if she wants a way to make sure she can't hurt anyone, to make sure that even if she becomes a terrifying monster that her ability to <i>do</i> anything about it will be severely curtailed?<br /> <br /> And if her unfinished business is important to her, maybe she dreads losing or burying the part of herself that cares about that, too. </div> </div> </div>The problem is that a lot of Geists don't really have unfinished business. The reason given for the Bargain in the book--one of the actually concrete-ish things--is that they do it so that they can experience life again. They do it so that they can encourage their Sin-eater to strive and struggle and get drunk and fuck and fight (all things that can regain plasm, according to a small paragraph only mentioned once). Most of them want to live simply for living's sake. They don't get &quot;shafted&quot; in the Bargain, they get more out of it than the Sin-eater, even. They give up control and autonomy because they want to <i>live</i>. That's an important thing, and this is the second time in only a few days that I've seen someone forget that. Geists go from being ephemeral entities, diaphanous and whole, to living, <i>physical</i> things. They revel in pain so much that they keep the Sin-eater from passing out even when they're chili on a broken skeleton. They go from corpus to flesh and blood and muscle and sinew and veins and hormones and cells and neurons and LIFE. They go from being shadows to being able to indulge in every surge of adrenaline, the heady mix of oxytocin that come from being in love, the flood of endorphines that come with every fist to the face. That's not getting &quot;shafted&quot; in my book. It may look like they get nothing in return, but they get a whole hell of a lot more. That's why they give their Sin-eater pals some bonus super powers. &quot;You can shoot fire with your brain, go fight crime or something; make sure to get punched a lot&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Enter the bargain. By ensuring beforehand that the only way she can really reliably interact with the mortal world is through an empowered <i>person </i>who presumably shares some of her values (or at least moreso than the thing she's about to become), she's sort of externalizing her conscience. Suddenly, it's okay that she's giving up the part of herself that recoils from the idea of burning infants, because she is going to be working with someone who will almost certainly say &quot;no, we're definitely not doing that&quot;. </div> </div> </div> The problem here is that even though the Geist has no actual mechanical power, it's still there, influencing the Sin-eater. It's the jaunty skeleton making lewd motions with its staff, it's the archangel telling you that justice must be done. There's even a <a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/product/63728/Dem-Bones-Geist-The-SinEaters\" target=\"_blank\">one act SAS</a> that features a Sin-eater driven mad by a Geist. And The Horned Fiend desperately wants to get a Sin-eater but he certainly isn't doing it for a Jimminy Cricket.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, by binding herself to someone whose entire <i>schtick </i>probably involves seeing and helping ghosts, she's ensuring that even if <i>she</i> stops caring about her unfinished business, her bound partner is strongly inclined to treat her as a puzzle and try to figure it out and address it. By turning herself into an enigma and then leaving herself somewhere that a puzzle-hungry sin-eater is bound to start wondering about her, she's seeing to it that in the long run someone will care about that even if she doesn't. </div> </div> </div>This is one of the biggest problems with Geist as it currently is. A Sin-eaters entire schtick doesn't even have to involve helping ghosts. There's nothing at all that stops them from ignoring ghostly business at all, and at least one of the Archetypes is built around abusing ghosts for your own gain, not necessarily helping them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Did this make any sense? And if so, thoughts? </div> </div> </div>Not really. But like I said, most of this is because there are severe problems with the Geist book in terms of focus and editing. It could be the goal of <i>a</i> Geist, but it's far from the intentions of <i>Geists</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my first Storyteller's Vault product: <a href=\"http://www.storytellersvault.com/product/438661/Pretty-Guardian-Faerie-Knights-A-Hunter-the-Vigil-Conspiracy\" target=\"_blank\">Pretty Guardian Faerie Knights</a><br /> Currently working on <b>Night Horrors: Pushing Daisies</b>. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/1517001-%F0%9F%91%BB-a-handful-of-sample-geists\" target=\"_blank\">Sample here</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2998-aspel","name":"Aspel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2998_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"403"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}